\subsection*{Before We Begin}
A software \"license\" is a legal permission that tells a user what they are allowed to do with a piece of software. It can limit how many computers the software may be installed on, or whether the user may modify the code. When a license is sold, the buyer pays for the right to use the program under those conditions. Understanding this idea makes it easier to see why buying a program once (a license) differs from paying repeatedly for access (a subscription). With that distinction clear, we can explore the main kinds of software and the ways companies earn money from them. 

\section*{Motivation}
Buying a car outright (license) versus using a car‑sharing service that charges per ride (subscription/SaaS).  Just as these two approaches give people different ways to enjoy transportation, software can be delivered in several business models that suit varied user needs and company goals.  Recognizing the strengths of each model helps us understand why a single product might be sold in more than one way.  

\subsection*{Intuition}
Think of software as a tool that can be packaged and offered to users in distinct flavors.  The first flavor is the type of software itself—whether it runs the computer (system software), helps you accomplish tasks (application software), or assists developers in building other programs (programming software).  The second flavor is how the maker decides to get paid: some charge once for a permanent right to use, others ask for a regular fee, some give a free version and charge for extra features, and still others rely on support or consulting around freely available code.  By separating “what the software does” from “how the software is monetised,” we can compare the benefits and challenges of each approach.  

\subsection*{Formal Definition}
\begin{description}
  \item[System Software] Software that manages computer hardware and provides foundational services.  Examples include operating systems, device drivers, and utility programs.
  \item[Application Software] Software that enables users to perform specific tasks.  It may be general‑purpose (e.g., web browsers) or tailored for a particular domain (e.g., airline reservation systems).
  \item[Programming Software] Software that supports the creation, testing, and maintenance of other software.  Examples are integrated development environments (IDEs) and debuggers.
\end{description}

\begin{description}
  \item[License‑based Model] The vendor sells a permanent right (a license) to install and use the software.  The buyer typically pays a one‑time fee, such as the $29.99 charge for a desktop photo‑editing program.
  \item[Subscription / SaaS Model] The vendor retains the software on its own servers (or provides regular updates) and charges the user a recurring fee for continued access, similar to a car‑sharing service that bills per ride.
  \item[Freemium Model] The vendor offers a basic version at no cost and charges for premium features or services.  The free tier provides enough value to attract users, while a subset of users chooses to pay for added capabilities.
  \item[Open‑source Model] The source code is publicly available, but the vendor may generate revenue through consulting, support contracts, or managed services.  Open‑source software is not necessarily free for the end‑user; costs can arise from the ancillary services.
  \item[Custom Development / Consulting Model] The vendor works on a project‑by‑project basis, delivering software that meets a specific client’s requirements in exchange for a contract fee.
\end{description}

Other revenue streams that may accompany any of the above models include advertising displayed within the software, platform fees charged to third‑party developers, and selling data‑analytics services.  It is important to note that open‑source products can still charge for value‑added services, that freemium premium versions contain additional functionality rather than hidden copies of the free features, and that subscription models are widely used for enterprise‑grade applications as well as consumer apps.  

\subsection*{Worked Example}
\textbf{Problem statement:}  
A hypothetical mobile app provides basic photo editing for free.  It offers premium filters for $4.99 per month under a freemium model.  Compare the projected annual revenue of this freemium app with a similar app that is sold once for a $29.99 license.  Assume there are 100 000 free users, and that 5 % of them convert to the premium plan.

\textbf{Step 1: Compute the number of premium users.}  
\(100\,000 \times 0.05 = 5\,000\) users convert to the premium plan.

\textbf{Step 2: Calculate monthly revenue from premium users.}  
\(5\,000 \times \$4.99 = \$24\,950\) per month.

\textbf{Step 3: Calculate annual revenue from the freemium model.}  
\(\$24\,950 \times 12 = \$299\,400\).

\textbf{Step 4: Compute revenue for the license‑based app.}  
Assume the same total market of 100 000 users each purchases the one‑time license.  
\(100\,000 \times \$29.99 = \$2\,999\,000\) in a single year (if all purchase within the year).

\textbf{Final answer:}  
The freemium app would earn approximately \(\$299{,}400\) per year, whereas the license‑based app could generate about \(\$2{,}999{,}000\) in the same period if all users buy the license.

\textbf{What this shows us:}  
A freemium model can produce substantial recurring revenue even when only a small fraction of users upgrade, but a pure license sale can yield much larger one‑time income if the product attracts a comparable user base.  The choice between models depends on factors such as expected conversion rates, market size, and the desire for ongoing cash flow.  

\subsection*{Key Takeaway}
Software can be grouped into system, application, and programming categories, and companies earn money through diverse models—license, subscription, freemium, open‑source services, and custom contracts—each shaping how value is captured from users.

