3.theory:
\subsection*{Before We Begin}
Copyright gives the creator of a work the right to decide how that work may be copied or altered.  Because a software program is a kind of literary work, its author can attach a licence that spells out the rules for sharing, studying, or modifying the code.  Licences therefore make it possible for many people to use the same program while still respecting the author’s wishes.  Understanding that a licence is a legal tool for controlled sharing helps explain why the Free Software Foundation introduced the GNU General Public License.  With that picture in mind we can look at how the open‑source movement grew.

\subsection*{Motivation}
A community garden where anyone can plant, tend, and harvest, versus a private backyard owned by one family.  Just as a shared garden encourages many hands to improve the plot, the open‑source model invites programmers worldwide to contribute, adapt, and redistribute software.  This approach solves the problem of isolated development—by pooling effort, bugs are found faster and features evolve more rapidly—while still giving creators a way to keep control over how their work is used.

\subsection*{Intuition}
The open‑source story is a timeline of people who decided that software should be a shared resource rather than a secret product.  Early researchers in the 1950s and 1960s freely exchanged code, a habit that continued when the Unix operating system was released from Bell Labs.  In the 1980s, a group led by Richard Stallman formalised this spirit with the GNU Project and the GPL, which guarantees four essential freedoms for every user.  Later, Linus Torvalds released the Linux kernel, showing that a large, usable system could be built entirely in the open.  The term “Open Source” was coined in 1998 to emphasise pragmatic licences that businesses could adopt without moral debate.  Today, repositories such as GitHub host millions of projects, and even the biggest technology firms contribute code, sponsor development, or acquire open‑source companies.

\subsection*{Formal Definition}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Major historical milestones}\\
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item 1950s–1960s: Researchers freely share source code in academic and industrial labs. 
      \item 1970s: Creation of the Unix operating system at Bell Labs spreads a culture of code exchange. 
      \item 1983: Richard Stallman launches the Free Software Foundation (FSF) and the GNU Project. 
      \item 1989: The GNU General Public License (GPL) is published, introducing the concept of \emph{copyleft}. 
      \item 1991: Linus Torvalds releases the Linux kernel, which quickly gains worldwide contributors. 
      \item 1998: The term “Open Source” is introduced by the Open Source Initiative (OSI), shifting focus to business‑friendly licensing. 
      \item 2000s–present: Major corporations (e.g., IBM, Microsoft, Google) join the ecosystem by contributing code, sponsoring foundations, and acquiring open‑source businesses such as Red Hat and GitHub. 
    \end{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Four software freedoms defined by the GPL}\\
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item Freedom 0: the freedom to run the program for any purpose. 
      \item Freedom 1: the freedom to study how the program works and change it, which requires access to the source code. 
      \item Freedom 2: the freedom to redistribute copies so others can benefit. 
      \item Freedom 3: the freedom to distribute modified versions under the same licence, ensuring the same rights downstream. 
    \end{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Difference between “Free Software” and “Open Source”}\\
    \begin{itemize}
      \item \textit{Free Software} (as advocated by the FSF) stresses the ethical claim that software users should have the four freedoms above; the word “free” refers to liberty, not price. 
      \item \textit{Open Source} (as promoted by the OSI) highlights practical advantages—such as faster development, higher quality, and reduced cost—while accepting the same licences that grant the four freedoms.  Thus the two terms describe the same set of licences but from different motivational angles. 
    \end{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Three common ways corporations participate in the modern OSS ecosystem}\\
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item \textit{Direct contribution}: engineers employed by the company submit code, bug fixes, or documentation to public repositories. 
      \item \textit{Sponsorship and funding}: firms provide monetary support to foundations (e.g., the Linux Foundation) or pay developers to work on critical open‑source projects. 
      \item \textit{Strategic acquisition}: companies purchase or merge with established open‑source firms (e.g., IBM acquiring Red Hat, Microsoft acquiring GitHub) to integrate open‑source technology into their product lines. 
    \end{enumerate}
\end{itemize}
These definitions make clear that open‑source software is not automatically free of charge, that projects are governed by licences, contributor covenants, and community processes, and that corporate involvement does not dissolve the openness of a project.

\subsection*{Key Takeaway}
Open source evolved from a culture of sharing code into a formal movement defined by the GPL’s four freedoms, and today both ideals and major corporations cooperate to keep software collaborative, transparent, and widely usable.

