5.theory:
\subsection*{Before We Begin}
A software module is a self‑contained piece of code that performs a specific task, and a function is a named block inside a module that can be called repeatedly.  When a piece of code is written once and then reused in many places, development time and errors are reduced.  Reusing modules and functions lets programmers avoid reinventing the wheel for common operations such as calculations or file handling.  This idea of reusable code is the foundation for both libraries and frameworks.  Understanding reuse will help us see why libraries and frameworks are introduced in software projects.  

\subsection*{Motivation}
A kitchen toolbox (library) that you pick specific tools from versus a fully equipped kitchen (framework) that guides how you cook the whole meal.  Modern software development needs rapid delivery and high quality, but writing every low‑level component from scratch is slow and error‑prone.  Libraries and frameworks provide ready‑made building blocks that let developers focus on the unique parts of their application.  By choosing the right kind of open‑source component, teams can boost productivity while maintaining control over the overall design.  

\subsection*{Intuition}
Think of a **library** as a shelf of individual tools—like a hammer, a wrench, or a calculator—that you may reach for whenever you need them.  You decide which tools to use, when to call them, and how they fit into the larger structure you are building.  A **framework**, on the other hand, is like a fully equipped kitchen where the layout, the order of steps, and the flow of cooking are largely predetermined.  You supply the specific recipes (your custom code), but the kitchen (the framework) decides when and how to invoke them, a pattern known as *inversion of control*.  In short, libraries offer optional helpers, while frameworks provide a guiding skeleton for your entire program.  

\subsection*{Formal Definition}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Software library}: a collection of modules and functions that an application may call directly.  Each module groups related functions (e.g., a \texttt{math} module for arithmetic operations).  The application retains full control over the program flow.
  \item \textbf{Framework}: a structured set of libraries together with a prescribed architecture that calls the developer’s code at defined points.  This calling pattern is called \textbf{inversion of control} because the framework, not the application, initiates the execution of user‑written functions.
  \item \textbf{Inversion of control}: the reversal of the usual relationship where the program invokes library code; instead, the framework invokes the programmer’s callbacks, route handlers, or component methods.
  \item \textbf{Open‑Source Software (OSS) categories} (representative projects):
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item Operating Systems – \texttt{Linux}, \texttt{Android}, \texttt{BSD}
      \item Web Servers – \texttt{Apache HTTP}, \texttt{Nginx}
      \item Big Data – \texttt{Hadoop}, \texttt{Spark}
      \item QA Automation – \texttt{Selenium}
      \item Web Frameworks – \texttt{Django}, \texttt{Angular}, \texttt{React}
      \item Databases – \texttt{MySQL}, \texttt{PostgreSQL}
      \item Browsers – \texttt{Firefox}, \texttt{Brave}
      \item Scripting Languages – \texttt{Python}, \texttt{Perl}
      \item Content Management Systems – \texttt{WordPress}
    \end{enumerate}
\end{itemize}
It is a common misconception that a framework is simply a larger library; unlike a library, a framework dictates the overall program structure and lifecycle.  Another mistake is assuming that importing a library automatically manages the flow of an application—control remains with the programmer.  Finally, not every OSS project works out‑of‑the‑box; many require configuration, integration, or adaptation to fit a specific project.  

\subsection*{Worked Example}
\textbf{Problem statement:} Write a short Python script that uses the \texttt{requests} library to fetch the web page \texttt{http://example.com}, then contrast it with a minimal Flask application that serves the same page using a framework.

\medskip
\textbf{Step 1 – Using a library (requests).}
\begin{verbatim}
import requests

def fetch_page():
    response = requests.get('http://example.com')
    if response.status_code == 200:
        print(response.text)
    else:
        print('Failed to retrieve page')

if __name__ == '__main__':
    fetch_page()
\end{verbatim}
The script explicitly calls \texttt{requests.get()} to perform an HTTP GET request, checks the status code, and prints the returned HTML.  Control of the program flow (when the request is made, how to handle the response) stays entirely in the script.

\medskip
\textbf{Step 2 – Using a framework (Flask).}
\begin{verbatim}
from flask import Flask, Response
import requests

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/')
def serve_page():
    resp = requests.get('http://example.com')
    return Response(resp.text, mimetype='text/html')

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run()
\end{verbatim}
Here the developer defines a route \texttt{'/'} and a handler function \texttt{serve\_page}.  The Flask framework creates the web server, listens for incoming HTTP requests, and invokes \texttt{serve\_page} whenever a client accesses the root URL.  This is inversion of control: Flask decides *when* to call the user‑written function, whereas in the library example the script decides *when* to call \texttt{requests.get()}.

\textbf{Final answer:} The library example is a straightforward, linear script that pulls a page on demand.  The framework example sets up a full web service where the framework controls request handling and calls user code at the appropriate moment.

\textbf{What this shows us:} A library supplies a specific capability that the program can invoke at will, while a framework supplies a broader structure that determines the program’s execution flow and calls user‑provided code via inversion of control.  

\subsection*{Key Takeaway}
A library offers optional, reusable functions that you call directly, whereas a framework provides a complete architectural skeleton that calls your code for you through inversion of control.  Choose a library when you need isolated helpers, and choose a framework when you want the surrounding structure to be managed for you.

