6.theory:
\subsection*{Before We Begin}
Continuous integration (CI) is a practice where developers frequently run automated tests on their code to catch problems early. An issue‑tracking system records bugs, feature requests, and other work items, assigning them to people and tracking their progress. Nightly builds are automatically generated versions of the software compiled each night after the latest tests have passed. These concepts help large, distributed projects keep the code healthy despite many contributors. Understanding CI and issue tracking will let us follow how VLC moves from a small patch to a tested build.

\subsection*{Motivation}
Imagine a student who discovers a typo in VLC’s help menu and wants to fix it for everyone who uses the player. By submitting a small change, the student can see the entire journey a contribution takes—from local testing on a personal computer, through peer review, to automated verification before the change appears in a nightly build. This concrete path shows why VLC has a structured workflow: it ensures quality, security, and coordination among many volunteers.

\subsection*{Intuition}
VLC is maintained by a modest core team that guides a large community of occasional contributors. Each change starts as a “patch”—a file describing what was added or altered. Before anyone else sees the patch, the author runs built‑in checks to verify it does not break existing functionality. The patch is then emailed to a maintainer, who reviews it and discusses any needed revisions. Once accepted, an automated system (Jenkins) builds the updated code and runs the full test suite; the resulting nightly build lets anyone test the newest code, even though it is not the official release. Milestones are selected by a vote of the core team, determining when a stable version is packaged for public download.

\subsection*{Formal Definition}
\begin{description}
\item[Core team] A small group of 10–15 developers who have commit access to the main repository and make decisions about milestones, releases, and governance.
\item[Contributors] Any individual who submits patches; they may have only read‑only access to the repository and do not need commit rights.
\item[Trac] An issue‑tracking system used to log bugs, feature requests, and tasks. Security vulnerabilities are handled outside Trac to avoid public exposure.
\item[GitLab] The platform that hosts VLC’s source code repositories; it allows cloning, branching, and viewing the project’s history.
\item[Patch] A text file produced by a version‑control diff command that records the exact changes made to source files.
\item[make check] A command that runs the project’s suite of local tests and static analyses; it must succeed before a patch is sent for review.
\item[Jenkins] A continuous‑integration server that automatically builds the project, runs all tests, and publishes nightly builds for testing.
\item[Nightly build] An automatically generated, frequently updated build that reflects the latest merged patches; it is intentionally unstable and meant for testing, not for end‑user distribution.
\item[Milestone] A planned collection of features and fixes that will become an official release once the core team votes to approve it.
\item[Voting] The process by which the core team decides on milestones and release contents; each core member casts a vote, and a majority determines the outcome.
\end{description}
These definitions make clear that open‑source projects like VLC do employ formal code review, that nightly builds are testing artifacts rather than final products, and that many contributors work without direct commit privileges.

\subsection*{Worked Example}
\textbf{Goal:} Submit a small help‑text correction to VLC and see it pass through the CI pipeline.

\paragraph{Step 1 – Clone the repository}
\begin{verbatim}
$ git clone https://gitlab.videolan.org/videolan/vlc.git
Cloning into 'vlc'...
... (output omitted)
\end{verbatim}
The local copy of VLC’s source code is now on the student’s machine.

\paragraph{Step 2 – Create a new branch for the change}
\begin{verbatim}
$ cd vlc
$ git checkout -b fix-help-typo
Switched to a new branch 'fix-help-typo'
\end{verbatim}
Working on a separate branch keeps the main line untouched.

\paragraph{Step 3 – Edit the help text}
Open \texttt{modules/help/help.txt} in an editor and change the line
\begin{verbatim}
“Press P to play/pause the video.”
\end{verbatim}
to
